 of the Bishop of Le Havre in 1974, when the diocese of Le Havre was created.","wpu":"Le Havre Cathedral","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Cath%C3%A9drale_Notre-Dame_du_Havre%2C_10_August_2021.jpg/960px-Cath%C3%A9drale_Notre-Dame_du_Havre%2C_10_August_2021.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Museum of Modern Art André Malraux - MuMa","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":49.485043,"lo":0.102616,"d":456,"ds":"The Museum of Modern Art André Malraux - MuMa (French: Musée d'art moderne André Malraux, also known as Musée Malraux or simply MuMa) is a museum in Le Havre, France containing one of the nation's most extensive collections of impressionist paintings. It was designed by Atelier LWD, an architecture studio led by Guy Lagneau, Michel Weill and Jean Dimitrijevic.","wpu":"Museum of Modern Art André Malraux - MuMa","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Le_havre_musee_int.jpg/960px-Le_havre_musee_int.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hotel Dubocage de Bléville","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":49.48896,"lo":0.114323,"d":539,"ds":"Hôtel Dubocage de Bléville is a department of the museum of Art and History in Le Havre, Normandy, France. The architectural complex consists of two jointed houses built in the 17th and 18th century and a French garden. At the moment the museum mostly houses temporary exhibitions in the building.","wpu":"Hotel Dubocage de Bléville","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Le_Havre_H%C3%B4tel_Dubocage_De_Bl%C3%A9ville_%28213965591%29.jpeg/960px-Le_Havre_H%C3%B4tel_Dubocage_De_Bl%C3%A9ville_%28213965591%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hôtel de Ville, Le Havre","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":49.4941,"lo":0.1078,"d":680,"ds":"The Hôtel de Ville (French pronunciation: [otɛl də vil], City Hall) is the local seat of government in Le Havre, Normandy, France. It was designated a monument historique by the French government in 2017.","wpu":"Hôtel de Ville, Le Havre","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/b7/Le_Havre_02.jpg/960px-Le_Havre_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Abbaye de Graville","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":49.50333,"lo":0.16561,"d":4563,"ds":"Abbaye de Graville (Abbey of Graville) is an abbey located in the city of Le Havre, in the Normandy region of France. The architectural complex was founded in the 11th century and consists of a church, conventual buildings, a refectory, a cloister, a garden, and a cemetery. Nowadays, one of the departments of the Museum of Art and History of Le Havre (Musée d'Art et d'Histoire du Havre) is located in the Abbey.","wpu":"Abbaye de Graville","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/LeHavreGraville_02.jpg/960px-LeHavreGraville_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Montivilliers Abbey","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":49.545,"lo":0.192778,"d":8860,"ds":"Montivilliers Abbey (French: Abbaye de Montivilliers; Latin: Monasterium Villare) is a former Benedictine nunnery, founded between 682 and 684 (684) by Saint Philibert in the town of Montivilliers in Normandy, in the present department of Seine-Maritime, France. It was suppressed during the French Revolution, but many buildings, including the church, have survived.","wpu":"Montivilliers Abbey","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Montivilliers_abbaye.JPG/960px-Montivilliers_abbaye.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"wk":"2026-10-18"}